    "lawCode": "Amd §3006, Tax L",
    "lawSection": "Tax Law",
    "memo": " BILL NUMBER:  S7225A\n\n TITLE OF BILL :\n\nAn act to amend the tax law, in relation to procedures involving\ntaxpayer interviews\n\n PURPOSE :\n\nAdds \"Enrolled Agent\" to the list of taxpayer representatives to be\nrecognized by the Department of Taxation and Finance.\n\n SUMMARY OF PROVISIONS :\n\nSection 1: Amends subdivision (b) of section 3006 of the Tax Law to\nadd \"enrolled agent\" to the section. This section of law allows a\ntaxpayer to suspend an interview from the Dept. of Taxation and\nFinance in order to request representation from an Enrolled Agent.\n\nSection 2: Establishes the effective date.\n\n JUSTIFICATION :\n\nAn Enrolled Agent is an authorized tax preparer licensed to practice\nand represent clients before the federal Internal Revenue Service\n(IRS). They also represent New York State taxpayers on state tax\nissues. The clients of Enrolled Agents often sign \"powers of attorney\"\nauthorizing their representation and directing the IRS to send all\ncommunications through the Enrolled Agent. Federal law and regulation\nrequires the IRS to communicate exclusively through the Enrolled\nAgent, and requires the IRS to respect those \"powers of attorney\".\n\nIn New York, Enrolled Agents have had difficulty with the NYS\nDepartment of Taxation and Finance respecting \"powers of attorney\",\nand continuing to communicate directly with the represented taxpayer.\nThis leads to frustration and mistrust with both Enrolled Agents\nworking in New York and their clients. It can also delay the\nresolution of these matters. This legislation adds \"Enrolled Agents\"\nto the list of representatives covered by Section 3006 (2) of the Tax\nLaw to ensure that when a taxpayer requests representation by an\nEnrolled Agent an interview with a Tax and Finance employee will stop.\n\n LEGISLATIVE HISTORY :\n\n2015: A.5359-B/Veto No. 231\n\n FISCAL IMPLICATIONS :\n\nNone\n\n EFFECTIVE DATE :\nImmediately.\n",
